//@ ignore
// REPRODUCTION: on the #1649 SoA arena branch, a compiler built from these
// sources cannot compile ANY program containing more than one function.
//
// Not run by the suite (`//@ ignore`): the suite compiles with a compiler that
// does not have this defect, so it would pass there and prove nothing.
//
// Build a compiler from this branch, then:
//
//   ./mad --native-v2-compile tests/known_failures/soa_arena_two_function_codegen.sio -O -o /tmp/x.elf
//
// Branch:            Error: Failed to write native binary ... rc=12
// origin/main:       rc=0, and the binary runs and exits 4
//
// rc=12 is `!compile_ok` at native/codegen_x86_linux.sio:10839 -- a generic
// backend failure with no diagnostic. Lowering SUCCEEDS first ("final_fn_count
// 2"), so the defect is in native codegen, downstream of the arena conversion.
//
// Bisected: one helper function is enough. A single-function program compiles
// and runs correctly on the branch, which is why the failure stayed invisible --
// the branch's self-compile is GREEN because the SEED (lean_single) builds it,
// and lean_single does not carry this defect. `exit 0` on the build says
// nothing about the compiler it produced. See scripts/dev/ir-arena/README.md.
//
// Same rc=12 blocks scripts/ci/ir_instr_arena_gate.sh, so the arena's runtime
// contract cannot be verified until this is fixed. That makes this the top
// blocker on the branch -- ahead of raising IR_MAX_INSTRS.
//
// Refs #1649, #1655

fn s0(x: i64) -> i64 { x * 2 + 1 }

fn main() -> i64 {
    var t: i64 = 1
    t = (t + s0(t)) % 1009
    t % 251
}
